Coronal Chop

Ingredients: DOOM METAL + SKA + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Vocoder + 1950s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: Descends from roving midway carnival musicians who performed exclusively during the transient totality of solar eclipses.

Coronal Chop is a hypnotic, down-tempo anomaly where the buoyant, syncopated offbeat chops of 1950s Jamaican ska and big-band horn charts are dragged down to a glacial 45-BPM doom crawl, suspended in vast chambers of EMT plate reverb and narrated by ghostly, early-prototype tube vocoders.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: Agonizingly spacious 42–54 BPM; the signature syncopated ska guitar skank lands cleanly on the offbeat, but hangs suspended in tape slapback over an earth-shaking, fuzz-saturated walking bassline slowed down to tectonic speeds.
- Harmonic Palette: Somber Aeolian and Dorian modes cross-pollinated with classic 1950s I–vi–IV–V doo-wop progressions transposed into drop-A tuning, offset by mournful, airy major-seventh brass swells.
- Production Signatures: Cavernous EMT 140 plate reverb chained into Ampex 350 tape slapback; early 1950s military-surplus carrier-wave vocoders processing multi-part barbershop vocal harmonies; dual-tracked clean archtop jazz guitars colliding with catastrophic silicon fuzz boxes.
- Dynamics: Extreme tidal contrasts where whispering, ethereal vibraphone chimes and hollow vocal airs suddenly collapse under monolithic, distorted horn hits and subterranean bass drops.

Scene & Origin Lore
The style germinated between 1954 and 1959 along the Gulf Coast carnival circuit, pioneered by a transient collective known as the Midway Astronomers. Composed of displaced Jamaican mento players, New Orleans brass veterans, and renegade radio repair technicians, the ensemble maintained a strict ritual: they never struck a single chord under ordinary sunlight, assembling their gear on wooden boardwalk piers only during the brief minutes of a solar eclipse. Dressing in salt-stained 1950s wool tuxedos and welder's goggles fitted with eclipse-safe smoked glass, their audiences gathered not to dance, but to sway in a collective, slow-motion trance called the "Totality Lean" while carnival rides ground to a silent halt overhead.

